{"_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "Source Code ", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 5, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 1, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "A1.2", "type": "level"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"content": "These are words you use to talk about Imperative Languages ", "type": "text"}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Understand key information about the concepts, principles, and methodologies in simple spoken and written situations.  Talk about programming techniques, use cases, kinds, and sequences in simple spoken and written situations.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Communicates effectively in English about the communicative topics for this level through the use of appropriate basic vocabulary and grammatical structures.  Identifies general aspects related to Software Programming in short written or spoken conversations about past experiences.", "type": "secondary_1"}], "type": "objectives"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob05re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Simple Past of Verb Be in Affirmative", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You use the <strong>Simple Past Tense</strong> to express actions that started and finished in the past.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "You can use the Past simple</strong> to talk about activities or situations that happened already. Use the verb <strong>to be</strong> in the past to talk about past time states, emotions, characteristics, and locations. The forms of the verb be in the <strong>past</strong> are <strong>was</strong> for singlular and non count nouns, and <strong>were</strong> for plural nouns.</br></br>  Examples: </br> <i>He <strong>was</strong> interested in the source code class. </br> We <strong>were</strong> at the systems lab. </br> That sofware <strong>was</strong> easy to set.</i> </br></br>  Look at the following chart to see the subjects and their verb be form in the past.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "{\"rows\":1,\"columns\":1,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Past of verb <strong>BE</strong>\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" intro_table_border instruction_table_background\"}", "type": "table"}, {"content": "{\"rows\":7,\"columns\":1,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"italic\\\">Affirmative</span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"I was a student.\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"You were an engineer.\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"He was in the school.\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"She was a student.\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"We were in the classroom.\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"They were students.\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" intro_table_border instruction_table_background\"}", "type": "table"}, {"content": "I recognized that the program was part of a source tree, and that the source code stored algorithms on the computer's hard disk as well as in a database. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "We were at Professor Michael's programming class.", "type": "example"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Simple Present Tense to Talk about General Truths", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You can use the <strong>Simple Present</strong> to refer to routines, daily events, and things that happen regularly, or for statements that are of common knowledge.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "When you express <strong>general truths</strong>, you always use the simple present as the statement is static and not likely to change in the future; for example, the sentence \u201cEach country has a capital city\u201d is a universal truth and it is stated in <strong>Simple Present.</strong> </br></br>  Notice that for the subject pronouns <strong>I, we, you and they</strong> the <strong>Simple Present form</strong> is equal to the infinitive, but for <strong>she, he or it </strong>you need to add<strong>s/es </strong>at the end of the verb. Remember that you need the auxiliary verb <strong>do/does</strong> (for the third person singular) for the negative and interrogative form. </br></br>  Example: </br> <i> They<strong> design</strong> new programs. </br>  They<strong> don't design</strong> new programs</br>  <strong>Do they design </strong> new programs? </br></br>                  He <strong>designs </strong>software. </br>  He <strong>doesn't design</strong> software</br>  <strong>Does </strong>Pedro <strong>design</strong> software?</i> </br></br>  Look at the following chart:", "type": "text"}, {"content": "{\"rows\":1,\"columns\":1,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Present Simple\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" intro_table_border instruction_table_background\"}", "type": "table"}, {"content": "{\"rows\":8,\"columns\":4,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Affirmative \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Negative  \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Questions\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"I/you/we/they\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"italic\\\">I find a new program.  You send an email.</span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"italic\\\">We don't send new products.</span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"italic\\\">Do they get products?</span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"he/she/it\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"italic\\\">He sells software</span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"italic\\\">She doesn't buy software. </span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"italic\\\">Does she get the products?</span>\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" intro_table_border instruction_table_background\"}", "type": "table"}, {"content": "The assembler or compiler transforms the source code into binary machine code that the computer can understand. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "What does the assembler do?", "type": "example"}, {"content": "I don\u00b4t remember that.", "type": "example"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "ordinary", "type": "name"}, {"content": "not different or special", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective) ", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: A source code is a collection of computer instructions in an easy computer language, as an ordinary text that facilitates the job of computer programmers.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob05aud01", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "enterprise ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a company with commercial interests", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: The computer of the enterprise where I work uses that source.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob05aud02", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "interpret ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to infer or understand", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Then the computer stores the machine code and interprets the source code for execution.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob05aud04", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "helpful", "type": "name"}, {"content": "something useful and of help", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective) ", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Computer p